  "text": "The investigation measured how the shear resistance, the pressure needed to make a dent (yield pressure), and the sliding resistance of four different synthetic polymers changed when the temperature was varied from extremely cold to moderately warm. Unlike metals, these polymers showed pronounced shifts in their strength characteristics across the temperature span, and any confusing influence from surface residues on sliding resistance was removed. According to the adhesion model, the friction coefficient should be roughly the ratio of shear resistance to yield pressure. The experiments showed that this ratio matched the measured friction for the first polymer, but the second and third polymers consistently gave higher friction than the ratio predicts, while the fourth polymer gave considerably lower values. The discrepancies are explained by either stronger contact points under high local pressure for the second and third materials or by an inherently low adhesive interaction at the sliding surface for the fourth material."
